>         /* Original code called
>          *   host_DelayHwInterrupt(CPU_TIMER_INT, EoiIntsPending, ...)
>          * to deliver all queued-up "missed" ticks as one batch.  Inside
>          * host_DelayHwInterrupt (nt_eoi.c:326) a positive CallCount
>          * triggers an immediate synchronous ica_hw_interrupt for the
>          * timer line.  Because TimerEoiHook is invoked from inside
>          * ica_eoi (via host_EOI_hook, ica.c:466) -- BEFORE ica_eoi's
>          * tail scan_irr (ica.c:472) -- that inline raise re-asserts
>          * IRR bit 0 with ica_count > 0, pinning the timer at the top
>          * of the priority order.  The next ica_intack therefore picks
>          * IRQ0 again instead of the keyboard's IRQ1 (or any other
>          * lower-priority IRQ) that is already pending in IRR.  Result:
>          * total keyboard starvation once the timer falls behind even
>          * by one tick.
>          *
>          * Behaviour difference vs HAXM/v86/ccpu:  on those backends
>          * the cpu_simulate loop is fast enough that EoiPending stays
>          * at 0/1 and this branch is almost never entered, so the bug
>          * never surfaced.  On WHP the round-trip overhead of
>          * WHvRunVirtualProcessor + the worker-thread cancel keeps the
>          * timer permanently behind, making this branch hot.
>          *
>          * Drop the accumulated backlog instead of batch-firing it.
>          * The BIOS tick counter drifts slightly when the loop falls
>          * behind (lost ticks), but the keyboard / cascade IRQs in IRR
>          * can now drain.  The heartbeat thread continues to fire one
>          * tick per heartbeat naturally, so steady-state behaviour
>          * matches the other backends.
>          */
